    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><mfenced><mi>ii</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;Sand Meyer reaction</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><mfenced><mi>iv</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;Gatterman reaction</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;Wurtz reaction</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><mfenced><mi>iii</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;Fittig reaction</p><p>The Wurtz reaction is a very useful reaction in the fields of organic chemistry and organometallic chemistry for the formation of alkanes.</p><p>In this reaction, two different alkyl halides are coupled to yield a longer alkane chain with the help of sodium and dry ether solution.</p>",
